@model NormalizationViewModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Normalization";
    var flat = Model.FlatLines;
    var byWorkOrder = flat.GroupBy(l => l.WorkOrderId).ToList();
    var customersById = Model.Customers.ToDictionary(c => c.Id);
    var materialsById = Model.Materials.ToDictionary(m => m.Id);

    // Live numbers for the "update anomaly" talking point.
    var busiest = flat.GroupBy(l => l.CustomerName).OrderByDescending(g => g.Count()).First();
    var seenCustomer = new HashSet<string>();
    var seenMaterial = new HashSet<string>();
    string Money(decimal? v) => v is null ? "" : "$" + v.Value.ToString("N2");
}

<h1 class="h3 mb-1">Normalization, using JobDesk's own data</h1>
<p class="text-muted">
    Every table on this page is built from the live database. The "before" tables are what you'd get
    if all of this lived in one spreadsheet. The "after" tables are the real JobDesk schema.
    Edit a customer's phone on the Customers page and reload to watch it change in one place.
</p>

@* ---------- Step 0 ---------- *@
<div class="card mb-4">
    <div class="card-header bg-white">
        <span class="badge text-bg-danger me-2">Before</span><strong>One spreadsheet row per work order</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body pb-0 small">
        <p>
            This is how a job list often starts out. The <em>Materials</em> cell holds a list, so you can't
            sum quantities, filter by material, or join on it without parsing text.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-sm table-bordered small mb-0">
            <thead class="table-light">
                <tr><th>WO</th><th>Title</th><th>Customer</th><th>Phone</th><th>City</th><th>Materials</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var g in byWorkOrder)
                {
                    var first = g.First();
                    var lines = g.Where(l => l.MaterialName != null).ToList();
                    <tr>
                        <td>@first.WorkOrderId</td>
                        <td>@first.WorkOrderTitle</td>
                        <td>@first.CustomerName</td>
                        <td>@first.CustomerPhone</td>
                        <td>@first.CustomerCity</td>
                        <td class="bg-danger-subtle">@(lines.Count == 0 ? "" : string.Join("; ", lines.Select(l => $"{l.MaterialName} x{l.Quantity:0.##} @ {Money(l.UnitCost)}")))</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

@* ---------- 1NF ---------- *@
<div class="card mb-4">
    <div class="card-header bg-white">
        <span class="badge text-bg-warning me-2">1NF</span><strong>Atomic values: one row per material line</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body pb-0 small">
        <p class="mb-2">
            <strong>Rule:</strong> every cell holds one value and there are no repeating groups.
            Split the material list so each line is its own row. The key is now <code>(WorkOrderId, Material)</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>New problem:</strong> customer and material facts are copied onto every line (highlighted).
            <strong>@busiest.Key</strong>'s phone and city are stored <strong>@busiest.Count()</strong> times here, but only once in
            <code>Customers</code>. Change the phone and you have to update @busiest.Count() rows. Miss one and the data disagrees with itself.
            That's an <em>update anomaly</em>.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-sm table-bordered small mb-0">
            <thead class="table-light">
                <tr><th>WO</th><th>Title</th><th>Status</th><th>Customer</th><th>Phone</th><th>City</th><th>Material</th><th class="text-end">Unit cost</th><th class="text-end">Qty</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var l in flat)
                {
                    var repeatCustomer = !seenCustomer.Add(l.CustomerName);
                    var repeatMaterial = l.MaterialName != null && !seenMaterial.Add(l.MaterialName);
                    var c = repeatCustomer ? "bg-warning-subtle" : "";
                    var m = repeatMaterial ? "bg-warning-subtle" : "";
                    <tr>
                        <td>@l.WorkOrderId</td>
                        <td>@l.WorkOrderTitle</td>
                        <td>@l.Status</td>
                        <td class="@c">@l.CustomerName</td>
                        <td class="@c">@l.CustomerPhone</td>
                        <td class="@c">@l.CustomerCity</td>
                        <td class="@m">@l.MaterialName</td>
                        <td class="@m text-end">@Money(l.UnitCost)</td>
                        <td class="text-end">@l.Quantity?.ToString("0.##")</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

@* ---------- 2NF + 3NF explanation ---------- *@
<div class="row g-4 mb-4">
    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-white"><span class="badge text-bg-info me-2">2NF</span><strong>No partial dependencies</strong></div>
            <div class="card-body small">
                <p><strong>Rule:</strong> 1NF, and every non-key column depends on the <em>whole</em> key, not just part of it.</p>
                <p>The key is <code>(WorkOrderId, MaterialId)</code>, but:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Title</code>, <code>Status</code>, and the customer columns depend only on <code>WorkOrderId</code>. They move to <strong>WorkOrders</strong>.</li>
                    <li><code>MaterialName</code> and <code>UnitCost</code> depend only on <code>MaterialId</code>. They move to <strong>Materials</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Only <code>Quantity</code> depends on both. It stays in the junction table <strong>WorkOrderMaterials</strong>.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="mb-0">Now a price change is one row in <code>Materials</code>, not every line that ever used that material.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-white"><span class="badge text-bg-success me-2">3NF</span><strong>No transitive dependencies</strong></div>
            <div class="card-body small">
                <p><strong>Rule:</strong> 2NF, and non-key columns depend on the key and <em>nothing but</em> the key.</p>
                <p>
                    After 2NF, <code>WorkOrders</code> would still carry <code>CustomerName</code>, <code>Phone</code>, and <code>City</code>.
                    Those depend on the customer, not on the work order: <code>WorkOrderId → CustomerId → Phone</code>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Move them to <strong>Customers</strong> and keep only the foreign key <code>WorkOrders.CustomerId</code>.
                    That also fixes the <em>insert anomaly</em>: Oak Ridge Church can exist before it has any jobs.
                </p>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    It fixes the <em>delete anomaly</em> too. Deleting a customer's last job no longer deletes the customer's phone number.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@* ---------- After ---------- *@
<div class="card mb-4">
    <div class="card-header bg-white">
        <span class="badge text-bg-success me-2">After</span><strong>The real JobDesk tables (3NF)</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body small pb-2">
        Each fact is stored once. Arrows are foreign keys: <code>WorkOrders.CustomerId → Customers.Id</code>,
        <code>WorkOrderMaterials.WorkOrderId → WorkOrders.Id</code>, <code>WorkOrderMaterials.MaterialId → Materials.Id</code>.
    </div>
    <div class="row g-3 px-3 pb-3">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <div class="fw-semibold small mb-1">Customers</div>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered small mb-0">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Id (PK)</th><th>Name</th><th>Phone</th><th>City</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var c in Model.Customers)
                    {
                        <tr><td>@c.Id</td><td>@c.Name</td><td>@c.Phone</td><td>@c.City</td></tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <div class="fw-semibold small mb-1">Materials</div>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered small mb-0">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Id (PK)</th><th>Name</th><th>Unit</th><th class="text-end">UnitCost</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var m in Model.Materials)
                    {
                        <tr><td>@m.Id</td><td>@m.Name</td><td>@m.Unit</td><td class="text-end">@Money(m.UnitCost)</td></tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <div class="fw-semibold small mb-1">WorkOrders</div>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered small mb-0">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Id (PK)</th><th>CustomerId (FK)</th><th>Title</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var w in Model.WorkOrders)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td>@w.Id</td>
                            <td title="@(customersById.TryGetValue(w.CustomerId, out var cu) ? cu.Name : "")">@w.CustomerId</td>
                            <td>@w.Title</td>
                            <td>@w.Status</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <div class="fw-semibold small mb-1">WorkOrderMaterials <span class="text-muted fw-normal">(junction table)</span></div>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered small mb-0">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>WorkOrderId (FK)</th><th>MaterialId (FK)</th><th class="text-end">Quantity</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var l in Model.Lines)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td>@l.WorkOrderId</td>
                            <td title="@(materialsById.TryGetValue(l.MaterialId, out var ma) ? ma.Name : "")">@l.MaterialId</td>
                            <td class="text-end">@l.Quantity.ToString("0.##")</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4">
    <div class="card-header bg-white"><strong>Why the junction table and foreign keys matter</strong></div>
    <div class="card-body small">
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>A work order uses many materials, and a material is used on many work orders. That's many-to-many, and it needs a junction table. <code>WorkOrderMaterials</code> also holds the one fact that belongs to the pair: <code>Quantity</code>.</li>
            <li>Foreign keys stop orphans. You can't add a line for work order 999 if it doesn't exist. Deleting a customer who still has work orders is blocked (<code>OnDelete(Restrict)</code> in <code>JobDeskContext</code>).</li>
            <li>The price of normalizing is joins. Reports join the tables back together, and that's exactly where the join-grain bug in <code>BUG.md</code> came from.</li>
            <li>
                <strong>Deliberate denormalization:</strong> real systems often copy <code>UnitCost</code> onto the line at the moment the job is quoted, so later price changes don't rewrite old invoices.
                That's a conscious trade-off for history, not an accident. JobDesk keeps it simple and doesn't do this.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>
